The task has four parts: complex numbers as types (`c64`, `c128`), the pure-data layout for complex
values, the debug check that a compact-element view cannot escape, and the `aggregateSlots` header
offset taken from `Struct`/`Tuple` instead of being written down again. The review items are
`docs/handover/reviews/H17-H22.md:62-75`; the relevant design principles are 5.1-5.4 and 10.5.

## 1. Types, literals and conversions

### 1.1 Representation

`c64` and `c128` are numbers in the language and `TypeCode`s in the core, but they are **boxed and
GC-traced**, like `string`:

- `TypeCode::Complex64 = Primitive | GC_Traced, 0x012`;
- `TypeCode::Complex128 = Primitive | GC_Traced, 0x013`.

`isPrimitive()` tests that the four upper flag bits are zero, so it is **false** for both. That is
intentional: a `c128` is 16 bytes and a `c64` would fit 8 bytes only as raw bytes, while every Camel
number is one kind of value (a slot or a traced object). Keeping both widths as the same traced
`Complex` object also means equality, printing, conversion and foreign handles have one code path.
Every numeric test that must include complex values asks `camel::core::type::isComplex(code)`; the
places that used `isPrimitive()` as a stand-in for "number" were audited (numeric operators, literal
fitting, array element classification, DLPack, equality, index semantics, LSP names).

`Complex` (`include/camel/core/rtdata/complex.h`) stores `double real_`, `double imaginary_` and a
`TypeCode`. `Complex::create` rounds both parts to f32 when the code is `Complex64`. `ComplexData`
(`include/camel/core/data/primary.h`) is the compile-time counterpart and rounds the same way.

### 1.2 Imaginary literals

The lexer emits a new token, `Imaginary`, for a decimal number immediately followed by `i` when the
next character is not an identifier character (`4i`, `4.5i`, `1e3i`). Hexadecimal, binary and octal
integers take no suffix (`0xFFi` is `0xFF` and the name `i`), and `4if` is `4` and `if`. A minus sign
is still the unary operator, so `-2i` is `-(2i)`.

`literal::Value` gained `Kind::Imaginary` and `Kind::Complex`; the exact representation has two
rational parts (`Rep::rational`, `Rep::imaginary`) and two negative-zero flags, and arithmetic between
literals is exact on both parts. `Value::fromImaginaryText` reads the body as a real and puts it in the
imaginary part, the real part being zero. `toData` builds a `ComplexData` whose parts are rounded once,
to the target width. `toString`/`toFloatText` write the complex form.

`LiteralType::Imaginary` was added to the AST and `pass_spec.cpp` rejects it as a parameter default
with "a complex number is not a parameter value".

### 1.3 Type from context (design principles 5.1)

The order is the normative one: the expected type, then the other operand of a binary operation, then
the default.

- `let z: c64 = 2i` makes the literal a `c64`; the complex value is rounded to f32 and fitting is
  checked (`ComplexData::convertTo` refuses a c64 target when either part does not fit an f32).
- In `z + 4i` with `z: c64`, the imaginary literal takes `c64` from the other operand.
- **With no context, an imaginary literal is a `c128`.** This follows 5.1 directly (line 271 names
  `c128` as the default for a complex literal), and it is the only default that does not lose the
  value the user wrote: `c128` holds every decimal literal that `float` holds, while `c64` would round
  a literal that the source does not mark as narrow. The same default applies to a folded expression
  with an imaginary part (`3 + 4i`, `(1+2i)*(3+4i)`), and to an array or tuple literal whose elements
  are imaginary with no declared element type.
- A complex literal where a real type is expected is `SemanticDiag::LiteralNotRepresentable` with the
  reason "a complex value does not become a real one; use re() or im()". There is no implicit
  conversion between variables, so this is only the literal path.
- Array widening: elements are tracked by `literal::Kind`. A mix with any imaginary element widens to
  `c128`; non-imaginary elements are rebuilt as `ComplexData` of that type (`literalNodeInType`). A
  mix of integer and real literals still widens to `float` as before.

### 1.4 Conversions

Explicit type-constructor calls only, in the existing style (`int(x)`, `float(x)`):

- `c64(x)` for `i32`, `int`, `f32`, `float`, `c64`, `c128`;
- `c128(x)` for the same sources.

A real value becomes the real part, the imaginary part is `+0`; `c128` to `c64` rounds both parts to
f32. There is no complex-to-real conversion and no complex-to-real `as`: `re(z)` and `im(z)` are the
explicit operations. `castSafetyFrom` still returns `Forbidden` for a complex source, and
`Type::castSlotFrom` is not involved. `ComplexData::convertTo` only handles a complex target.

## 2. Arithmetic, equality and printing

`c64` and `c128` take the operators the numeric tables give every number, in pairs of static
functions with the established suffixes: `c` is `c64`, `z` is `c128` (`:op/add_c`, `:op/add_z`, ...).

| Operator | Result | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `+`, `-`, `*`, `/` | same complex type | IEEE parts, `std::complex<T>` arithmetic; a zero divisor is `RuntimeDiag::DivisionByZero`, not infinity |
| unary `-` | same complex type | negates both parts |
| `==`, `!=` | bool | both parts compared; IEEE semantics for NaN and zeros |
| `abs(z)` | `f32` for `c64`, `float` for `c128` | `std::hypot(real, imag)` |
| `re(z)`, `im(z)` | `f32` / `float` | the parts |
| `conj(z)` | same complex type | negated imaginary part |
| `exp(z)` | same complex type | `std::exp(std::complex<T>)` |

What is **not** there and why:

- **No ordering** (`<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=`). Complex numbers have no order. The numeric table simply has
  no complex entry for these groups, and constant folding returns "no value" when either side has an
  imaginary part, so the ordinary "no matching function" report is produced.
- **No `%`, no `^`** for complex. Both are declared per pair for real numbers only; the numeric
  semantics table (`src/builtin/executors/builtin_semantics.cpp`) declares only add/sub/mul/div/neg for
  the two complex types.
- **No Map/Set key.** Complex is content-comparable but has no order, so `collectionKeyProblem` rejects
  it with the existing "no order" reason. No special case was added.
- The numeric laws for complex declare `neutralBits = nullopt` (zero has a sign in each part, so a
  neutral value cannot be represented as bits), keep add and mul commutative, mul distributes over
  add, and keep sub as the subtraction of add. Neg is `NumericClass::Exact` with no compute kind.
- The `math` module's complex functions have no entry in `modules/math/semantics.cpp`: their numeric
  contract is intentionally opaque for now (see open questions).

Printing follows the existing number-text helpers: real part, `+` or `-`, the magnitude of the
imaginary part, `i` (`complexText` in `include/camel/utils/number_text.h`). A part with a whole finite
value writes no `.0`; each part of a `c64` uses the shortest text that reads back as that `f32`. A NaN
imaginary part is written with `+`. Examples: `3+4i`, `0+4.5i`, `0-2i`, `-1+0i`, `3-4i`.

`string(z)` uses the same text. A format specification for a complex value (`{:.2f}`) is not supported;
see open questions.

## 3. Pure-data layout and `slotShaped` by type

`DataLayout` gained the type-level leaf property `slotResident`:

```cpp
struct DataLayout {
    size_t size, alignment;
    bool aggregate;
    TypeCode scalar;
    size_t fieldCount;
    size_t *fieldOffsets;
    bool slotResident; // <- new: the bytes are one slot's bytes and are read as one slot
    bool slotShaped;
};
```

- `slotResident` is true for `i32`, `i64`, `f32`, `f64`, `bool`, `byte` (leaf scalar elements that the
  array slot fast path may read directly). It is false for complex values and for aggregates.
- `c64` layout: `{8, 4, false, Complex64, 0, nullptr, false, false}`.
- `c128` layout: `{16, 8, false, Complex128, 0, nullptr, false, false}`.
- `computePure` is true for `Complex64`/`Complex128`, so complex values are pure data (design
  principles 10.5) and may be stored compactly in an array or an aggregate without references.
- **`slotShaped` is computed from the field types, not from the size.** For an aggregate it is true
  only when every field is `slotResident`, not an aggregate, exactly 8 bytes, and at offset `i * 8`.
  A `c64` field is 8 bytes but is not slot-resident, so `{x: c64}` is not slot-shaped; it follows the
  general field loop with `fieldOffsets`, and a `c64` field is written/read as two f32 parts.
  This is the review's requirement (H28 review item 3): the decision is made by type.
- `aggregateSlots` and `aggregateSlotsMut` now live in `include/camel/core/rtdata/aggregate.h`, which
  includes `struct.h` and `tuple.h` and asserts at compile time that the two data offsets agree.
- Array element classification treats `Complex64`/`Complex128` as compact numeric elements:
  `isCompactElementType` is true, `arrayElementSize` is 8 and 16, and the array storage decision is
  unchanged otherwise (inline for a small array, block-backed after promotion, a view shares the
  block). `Array::getSlotSlow` has explicit complex branches **before** the size-8 slot branch, so a
  `c64` element is never read as a slot. `setSlot`'s slot fast path requires
  `isSlotResidentElement()`, so complex elements go through `setSlotSlow`, which boxes a `Complex`.
  `readLeaf`/`writeLeaf`/`materializeAggregate`/`storeAggregate` handle complex as two parts, including
  complex fields nested inside structs and tuples, and `compactEqualsElements` compares complex values
  by value (not by bit pattern) so `-0.0` and NaN follow `==`.
- DLPack: complex is exported/imported as `{code 5, bits 64 or 128, lanes 1}`, matching the DLPack
  complex encoding; zero-copy import stores the same two parts.

## 4. Views must not escape (H28 review item 1) — debug check

The H28 element view (`Array::getElementView` for a compact aggregate) is a `Struct`/`Tuple` marked as
a view into the array's bytes. It is not a value that can be stored. There are two layers:

1. **Always compiled: the detector and the store-path materialisation.**
   `isViewSlot(slot_t, const Type *)` in `include/camel/core/rtdata/view.h` asks whether the slot is
   exactly a view of that aggregate type (code check first, then `isView()` on the object). The store
   paths already materialise a view before copying (`materializeIfView`), so the normal path stores a
   real value.
2. **Debug builds only: the assertion.** `assertStoredValueIsNotView(slot, type, where)` in
   `src/core/rtdata/view.cpp` calls `ASSERT(condition, message)` with a message that names the
   interface (`std::format`). `CAMEL_ASSERT_NOT_VIEW(slot, type, where)` is a macro over it. In a
   release build (`NDEBUG`, which is what `npm run build` produces) the `ASSERT` expands to
   `((void)0)`: **release builds do not check**, as the task requires.

`CAMEL_ASSERT_NOT_VIEW` is placed at every interface that can store a value:

| Interface | File | `where` |
|---|---|---|
| struct field | `include/camel/core/rtdata/struct.h` | `"struct field"` |
| tuple element | `include/camel/core/rtdata/tuple.h` | `"tuple field"` |
| array element (slot write reachable from the slot-shaped path) | `src/core/rtdata/array.cpp` (`setSlotSlow`) | `"an array element"` |
| closure capture | `src/execute/graph_runtime_support.cpp` (`writeRuntimeFillSlots`) | `"a closure capture"` |
| extension/function slots (FILL into tuple / array / struct) | same | `"a FILL into a tuple"` / `"an array"` / `"a struct"` |
| external interfaces (foreign rooted/pinned handle) | `src/core/mm/foreign_handle.cpp` | `"a foreign rooted handle"` / `"a foreign pinned handle"` |

The assertion is after `materializeIfView` where the store path materialises; at a store that receives
a slot from an untrusted interface it runs before the write.

### Exercising the check

`std::gc::views` (alias `std::gcviews`) is a new pass, `GcViewsPass` in `src/execute/pass.cpp`, which
runs `debugRunViewSelfTest()` (`src/core/rtdata/view_selftest.cpp`) and prints one line:

```text
VIEW_SELFTEST ok normal=clean escape=caught
```

The self-test builds a compact `Array` of `{f64 f, i32 i}`, fills it, takes `getElementView(0)` and
checks:

- after `materializeIfView`, `isViewSlot(materialized, structType)` is false (`normal=clean`);
- the untouched view is `isViewSlot(view, structType)` true (`escape=caught`).

The second line is the detector predicate the debug assertion uses, so the plan observes the same
condition in a release build where the `ASSERT` itself is compiled out. The plan also runs the
program through `std::gc::views` and its alias.

## 5. `aggregateSlots` header offset (H28 review item 2)

The hand-computed `sizeof(void*) + 2 * sizeof(uint32_t)` is gone. `Struct` and `Tuple` each expose

```cpp
static constexpr size_t slotDataOffset() noexcept { return sizeof(Struct); }
```

with `static_assert(sizeof(Tuple) == Tuple::slotDataOffset())` and
`static_assert(alignof(Struct) == alignof(slot_t))`; `include/camel/core/rtdata/aggregate.h` asserts
that the two class offsets are equal, and `aggregateSlots`/`aggregateSlotsMut` use
`Struct::slotDataOffset()`. The layout of the header is now defined once, in the class that owns it.

